    "question": {
      ":question/id": "54a70680-18d7-552a-b461-554632ff8182",
      ":question/problem": "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 12 & 11 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 9 & 6\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 12 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 38 & 29 \\\\ 0 & -51 & -49\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nBy applying elementary row operations of the form $R_{i}:=R_{i}+kR_{1},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary row operations?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make the entries below $a_{11}=-1$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 12 & 11 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 9 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n\\xrightarrow{R_{2}:=R_{2}+3R_{1}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 12 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 38 & 29 \\\\ -5 & 9 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n\\xrightarrow{R_{3}:=R_{3}-5R_{1}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 12 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 38 & 29 \\\\ 0 & -51 & -49\\end{bmatrix}=B\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary row operations are\n\n$R_{2}:=R_{2}+3R_{1}$ and $R_{3}:=R_{3}-5R_{1}$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
